\section{Introduction}
Flaaitaal (also Tsotsitaal, Fly Taal, Isicamtho) is a South African urban speech variety, originally Afrikaans-based in structure with vocabulary from Setswana, isiXhosa, isiZulu and other languages. Recent scholarship characterises it as a register or style rather than a language with an ethnic community. Some sources describe it as moribund in the townships, with older speakers. This dossier is short because few educational materials were found.

\section{Primary Educational Resources}
No primers, textbooks or school programmes for Flaaitaal were found in the searches.

\section{Linguistic and Reference Materials}
\begin{itemize}
\item Overview article, Tsotsitaal and Camtho (Wikipedia): \url{https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Tsotsitaal_and_Camtho}
\item Dictionary of South African English entry ``flaaitaal'': \url{https://www.dsae.co.za/entry/flaaitaal/e02397}
\item Rosetta Panglossia entry for Fly: \url{https://rosettapanglossia.longnow.org/index.php?title=Fly}
\item Academic item at Wits WiredSpace (content not verified): \url{https://wiredspace.wits.ac.za/items/0439b601-a27a-42e6-8342-97895c739782}
\item Literator journal article (content not verified): \url{https://literator.org.za/index.php/literator/article/view/1274/2133}
\end{itemize}

\section{Religious and Community Resources}
None found.

\section{Digital and Audio Resources}
None found beyond the references above. Search results mention portrayals on South African television, but no specific archive was identified.

\section{Curriculum Development Context}
Flaaitaal has no ethnic community and is not known to be taught. A curriculum would most plausibly be a sociolinguistic awareness unit within Afrikaans or other South African language classes, drawing on the academic literature above.
